course: dictionary
lesson: 8576
title: A split in a group
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A schism is a split. When a religious group splits into two groups because people disagree, that is a schism. The sentence «In 1054 the churches of the East and the West split in what is called the Great Schism» gives you the clue: the word split comes right after the name, so schism must mean a split.
explain: The word is often used with a special name, «the Great Schism». Capital letters mean it is the name of one particular event, not just any split. You can also «cause a schism», which means to start a split that did not exist before.
explain: In serious or formal writing, a schism can also be any big split between members of a group, such as a club or a political party. To check your meaning, swap split into the sentence. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A schism is a split between groups, often because of disagreements.
fact: «The Great Schism» is the split of 1054 between the churches of the East and the West.
fact: The Eastern church is called Orthodox, and the Western church is called Catholic.
fact: «A schism in the church» means a split in a religious group.
fact: Schism is a formal word, so you may meet it in history lessons or in serious writing.
exercise: «In 1054 the churches of the East and the West split in what is called the Great Schism.» What is a schism? | A kind of prayer | A large party with music and food | *A split, where a group divides into two | A type of old church building
exercise: Which word in «The oldest split came in 1054, the Great Schism» is the clue that schism means a split? | Great | oldest | came | *split
exercise: Two groups form inside a club after a big argument. In formal writing, what might this be called? | A harvest | A feast | A festival | *A schism
test: What does «cause a schism» mean? | To stop an argument with a hug | To build a new church | To paint a picture of a group | *To start a serious split
test: «The Great Schism» split which two parts of the church? | Two villages by a river | *The Eastern (Orthodox) and the Western (Catholic) churches | Two schools in the same town | Two football teams
exam: Which sentence uses schism correctly? | She ran a schism around the park every morning | The schism was a tasty cake served at the party | *The club had a schism when half its members left to form a new club after a bitter disagreement | The sky had a schism of bright blue and gold
